@namespace ClimateExplorer.Web.Client.Components.RecentObservations

<div class="trends-overview">
    <p>
        A trend here is a simple (ordinary least squares) linear regression: the straight line
        through a set of yearly values that minimises the sum of the squared vertical distances
        from each year's value to the line. See
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simple_linear_regression" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikipedia: Simple linear regression</a>
        for the full mathematical background.
    </p>

    <h3>Why rates are shown per decade</h3>
    <p>
        The underlying calculation works in whatever
        units the data uses - degrees per year, millimetres per year - but a per-year number is small
        enough to look like noise. Multiplying by ten to get a per-decade rate makes the number large
        enough to read at a glance.
    </p>

    <h3>Why some trends say "No significant trend" instead of a number</h3>
    <p>
        Every slope
        estimated from real, noisy data comes with uncertainty - a p-value, the probability of seeing
        a slope this far from zero by chance alone if the true long-term trend were actually zero.
        This site treats a slope as significant when that p-value is below 0.05.
        When a trend doesn't clear that bar, showing a specific number such as
        "+0.08°C per decade" would overstate confidence the data doesn't support, so the tile shows
        "No significant trend" instead.
    </p>

    <h3>How the slope itself is worked out</h3>
    <p>
        Picture each year in the record as a point:
        <em>x</em> is the year, <em>y</em> is that year's value (temperature, rainfall, whatever the
        metric is). The line's slope, written β ("beta"), comes from this formula:
    </p>

    <SlopeFormulaFigure />

    <ul>
        <li>x̄ is the average year across the record</li>
        <li>ȳ is the average value</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Working through it: for every year, take how far that year is from the average year (x − x̄),
        and how far that year's value is from the average value (y − ȳ), and multiply the two together.
        Add up that product for every year in the record - that's the sum on top. The sum on the bottom
        does the same thing with just the years: how far each year is from the average year, squared,
        and added up across the whole record. Divide one sum by the other and the result is the slope -
        how much the fitted line rises for each extra year.
    </p>

    <h3>A small worked example</h3>
    <p>
        Say a station has three years of data: 2001 at 14.00°C,
        2002 at 14.05°C, 2003 at 14.10°C. The average year is 2002 and the average temperature is 14.05°C.
    </p>

    <table class="trends-slope-example">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Year (x)</th>
                <th scope="col">Temp °C (y)</th>
                <th scope="col">x − x̄</th>
                <th scope="col">y − ȳ</th>
                <th scope="col">(x−x̄)(y−ȳ)</th>
                <th scope="col">(x−x̄)²</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>2001</td><td>14.00</td><td>−1</td><td>−0.05</td><td>0.05</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2002</td><td>14.05</td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.00</td><td>0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2003</td><td>14.10</td><td>1</td><td>0.05</td><td>0.05</td><td>1</td></tr>
            <tr class="trends-slope-example-sum"><td colspan="4">Sum</td><td>0.10</td><td>2</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
        The slope is the sum on top divided by the sum on the bottom: 0.10 ÷ 2 = 0.05°C per year - the
        +0.5°C per decade the tile would display, once that per-year figure is multiplied by ten as
        described next.
    </p>

    <h3>Limitations of linear regression</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <h4>Non-linearity</h4>
            <p>
                Ordinary least squares forces a single, constant rate of change
                onto the whole record, but warming (or drying, or any other trend) doesn't necessarily happen at
                a constant pace. It can accelerate, plateau, or reverse over different stretches of the same
                record. A single slope fitted across a century of data can hide a period of little change early
                on followed by faster change more recently - or the reverse. The fitted line is the best
                <em>straight-line</em> summary of the data, not proof that the underlying process is actually
                linear.
            </p>

            <p>
                The site offers three time windows - <strong>full period</strong>, <strong>last 30
                years</strong>, and <strong>early period</strong> (the first half of the record).
                Comparing the slope across these windows is a simple way to check for
                non-linearity: if the last-30-years rate is noticeably steeper than the early-period rate, the
                full-period figure is averaging over a change of pace rather than describing a single, unchanging
                rate. If the three windows broadly agree, that's evidence the trend has been reasonably constant.
                Neither window is "more correct" than the others - they're different lenses on the same data, and
                the gap between them is often as informative as any one number on its own.
            </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
            <h4>Sensitivity to endpoints</h4>
            <p>
                Because the line is fitted to minimise squared
                distances, a single unusually hot, cold, wet or dry year at either end of a short window can
                shift the slope noticeably - short windows are more exposed to this than long ones.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <h4>Autocorrelation</h4>
            <p>
                Consecutive years in a climate record aren't independent -
                a warm year tends to be followed by another warm year. The standard p-value calculation
                assumes independent observations, so it can understate the true uncertainty in a slope.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <h4>Extrapolation</h4>
            <p>
                The fitted line describes the record it was fitted to. Using
                it to project temperatures, rainfall, or anything else beyond the edges of the available data
                assumes the same rate continues indefinitely, which the data itself cannot confirm.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <h4>Missing or uneven data</h4>
            <p>
                Gaps or an uneven spread of years across the record
                can pull the fitted line away from what a complete record would show, especially over shorter
                windows.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What's on these pages</h3>
    <p>
        The tile's own rows only ever show the per-decade
        rate, or "No significant trend" - nothing else. R², the p-value, confidence intervals, the
        fitted equation, and everything else in a full statistical breakdown are only available here,
        one metric and time window at a time.
    </p>
</div>
